+Automatic shader priming keeps waiting while the foreground commit-rate gate
+is busy; elapsed time alone no longer starts a compile. The existing two quiet
+seconds still release a busy wait. Function preparation and periodic directory
+scan checkpoints share that gate. Cache identity, completed-function records,
+shader inputs, background QoS and the initial90-second delay remain unchanged.
+The wait function is checked with controlled commit/clock schedules, including
+the previous10-second busy release; foreground performance needs hardware data.
+
+
+The diagnostic driver phase hook writes bounded slow-call groups into the existing
+frame trace. Groups identify the Vulkan call, thread, inclusive and exclusive
+phase wall times and the call's thread CPU time. Only calls of at least8ms are
+retained, up to4096 groups of33 records (72bytes each). Incomplete groups remain
+unknown. CPU query overhead stays in the measured interval; non-running wall
+time includes both waits and descheduling. No new USB protocol or Native ABI.
+
+The local Darwin KMD retains one recording connection and caps each fence
+request at 1 ms without changing its absolute deadline. Normal BO allocation
+and host import collect completed resources but do not evict aged cache items.
+Successful fence observers claim at most one old item, then release its native
+mapping, lease and storage outside the retirement mutex. A reserved item cannot
+be borrowed or released by another collector. Busy or failed releases publish
+their partial unmap progress while retaining ownership. Memory-pressure and
+allocation-failure flushing remain immediate.
+
+CPU map/unmap use diagnostic phases 31/32, within 33 records per group. A map
+outside a profiled draw/pass creates a MapBuffer group (API 7, command 0); a
+nested map stays in its existing parent group. These wall times include waits
+and scheduling. Host checks cover routing and lifetime; latency needs hardware
+comparison.
+
+
+The initial GMEM tile-store command-stream reservation now returns its Vulkan
+error before entering unchecked packet emission. EndRenderPass frees its
+temporary state and skips later GPU commands; EndCommandBuffer returns the
+existing recording error before appending cache-flush packets. This handles
+the observed null write after a failed BoMap, while preserving the underlying
+mapping failure and032 cache policy. It is not a general GPU reset or a claim
+that every command-stream allocation failure has been handled.
+
+
+Synchronous Metal resource, function and pipeline creation now uses the existing
+compact frame span records (kinds22–29, retained at250us), including nested
+descriptor/runtime/reflection work. Outer calls record Mach THREAD_BASIC_INFO CPU time (1us precision)
+through the previously unused type18 c/d fields, with tid=1 only for a valid
+monotonic CPU delta; older and unmeasured rows retain zero. This distinguishes
+caller work before an encode batch is flushed from work waiting on the encode
+thread. It does not change batching, scheduling, GPU commands or shader caches.
+The optional pipeline journal no longer queries task/thread statistics when it
+is disabled. Hardware latency improvement remains to be measured.
+
+`ndt=1`, with frame diagnostics and a matching native GOP timing endpoint, records raw display counters and hardware timestamps immediately before and after each GPU scanout copy. Format-2 record 26 retains the complete bounded reply in numbered fragments. The source surface handle, kernel object identity and mapping-cache ID are separate fields. Inactive/stale clocks, missing fragments, non-GPU copies and uncertain presentation remain explicit; these records do not turn a software completion into a vblank. GPU034 synchronous API and thread-CPU timing stays enabled for attribution of confirmed long intervals.
+
+
+With display timing enabled, the render server also observes each registered
+CoreAnimation command-creation thread at25/60/110/180ms after the latest
+successful scanout copy. Type27 fragments retain Mach run state, cumulative
+CPU, PC/LR/SP/FP, up to8 frame links, all API errors and observation times.
+Type28 retains current loaded image UUIDs, bases and slides for local decoding.
+The sampler is bounded to8 threads and4096 samples; normal frames perform no
+Mach state query. thread_get_state temporarily stops its target internally;
+stack reads occur after return and matching registers are only a consistency
+check, not an atomic stack guarantee. Observations are joined to independently
+verified physical display intervals and do not themselves measure a slow frame.
+Shared-event waits and encodeDrain now use the existing wall-only span kinds30/31.
+Rendering, deadlines, callbacks, queue policies and synchronization are unchanged.
+
+
+The iPhone24A446 observer records the display server's next-update state and
+Mach display timer fields only for the verified QuartzCore UUID and the original
+IOMFBServer primary layout. AccelServer uses that same primary IOMFBServer
+prefix in this exact image: its unadjusted destructor calls IOMFBServerD2 and
+its update virtual slot is inherited. The reader accepts these two exact
+address points, strips data and callback PAC through their proper keys, and
+still verifies the display and timer callback identities. The state wire
+format and read count are unchanged; other subclasses remain rejected. It only reads memory; repeated fields report consistency, not
+atomicity. The encoding worker joins the registered render-thread observations,
+bounded to6144 samples. SpringBoard's actual main thread uses the same Mach/stack
+reader at20ms for at most ten minutes in its own springboard-waits.trace. Both
+files record kern.bootsessionuuid, mach_timebase_info and image UUID/base/slide; cross-
+process attribution requires the same boot and a common uptime clock.
+
+Per-Vulkan-call Mach CPU profiling is now opt-in with
+NATIVE_METAL_DRIVER_CPU_PROFILE=1. By default the old phase hook is not installed.
+Type14 batch start/end records retain cumulative thread CPU nanoseconds in t3,
+the query wall microseconds in cpu0, and validity bit0 in tid2. These query
+brackets are part of the observation cost; CPU deltas are not GPU duration.
+Type31 reports the actual profile-hook policy. Rendering calls and synchronization
+are unchanged; performance and display scheduling still require device evidence.
+
+Freed runtime-owned buffer backing uses the existing spare cache, with its
+64 MiB buffer budget, ten-second expiry and pressure release. Foreground
+allocations can reuse the original mapping and address object before another
+native allocation. Imported resources retain their owner and release ordering.
+
+Pipeline object collection processes only objects whose last holder was released
+since the preceding collection, using the upstream runtime release queue. It
+retains the exact object keys, destruction order and live shader/layout holds.
+
+The display wait reader accepts only the two verified primary server classes
+and the exact IOMFBDisplay or AppleWirelessDisplay identity in QuartzCore
+76ce2a6e. The latter's original destructor reaches the IOMFBDisplay base without
+adjusting this; the reader accesses no fields of the derived display. Discovery
+checks at most sixteen returned display objects and prefers the exact base
+identity. It retains the selected class in decoded evidence, so an observed
+wireless display is not represented as a physical panel.
